hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"15644","Yunus ibn Muhammad said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Asri narrated to me, Shihab ibn Abbad narrated to us, that he heard some of the delegation of Abd al-Qays, while they were saying: We came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and their joy with us was great. When we reached the people they made room for us and we sat, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, welcomed us and supplicated for us, then looked at us and said: Who is your chief and leader. So we all pointed to al-Mundhir ibn Aidh. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Is this al-Ashajj. It was the first day this name was put on him, by a blow to his face by the hoof of a donkey. We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. So he stayed behind after the people, and tied their mounts and gathered their goods, then brought out his bag and cast off his travel clothes and wore good clothes of his, then came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had stretched his leg and reclined. When al-Ashajj came near him the people made room for him and said: Here, O Ashajj. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said, and sat upright and drew back his leg: Here, O Ashajj. So he sat at the right of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he welcomed him and was gentle to him, and asked him about his land, and named for him village by village: al-Safa and al-Mushaqqar and other villages of Hajar. So he said: By my father and mother, O Messenger of Allah, you know the names of our villages better than us. So he said: I have trodden your land and it was opened for me therein. He said: Then he turned to the Ansar and said: O company of Ansar, honor your brothers, for they resemble you in Islam, most like to you in hair and skin, they submitted willingly, not coerced nor wronged, when a people refused to submit until they were killed. He said: When morning came he said: How did you see the honor of your brothers to you and their hospitality to you. They said: The best brothers, they softened our bedding and made our food good, and spent night and morning teaching us the Book of our Lord, Blessed and Exalted, and the Sunnah of our Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. So it pleased the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he rejoiced at it. Then he came to us man by man, and we presented to him what we had learned and knew, and among us was one who knew the greetings and the Mother of the Book and a surah and two surahs and the practices. Then he faced us and said: Is there with you any of your provisions. So the people rejoiced at that and hastened to their saddles, and every man among them came with a bundle of dates and placed them on a leather mat before him. So he pointed with a palm stalk in his hand, about a cubit and less than two cubits, which he used as a staff, and said: Do you call this al-Tadud. We said: Yes. Then he pointed to another bundle and said: Do you call this al-Sarfan. We said: Yes. Then he pointed to a bundle and said: Do you call this al-Burni. We said: Yes. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Indeed it is the best of your dates and most beneficial for you. He said: So we returned from that delegation of ours and increased planting of it, and our desire for it grew, until most of our palms and our dates became al-Burni. Al-Ashajj said: O Messenger of Allah, our land is heavy and unhealthy, and if we do not drink these drinks our colors change and our bellies swell. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Do not drink in gourd and green jar and hollowed stump, and let one of you drink in a waterskin tied over its mouth. Al-Ashajj said to him: By my father and mother, O Messenger of Allah, permit us in like this, gesturing with his hands. He said: O Ashajj, if I permit you in like this, gesturing with his hands thus, you would drink it in like this, spreading his hands and extending them, meaning greater than it, until when one of you becomes intoxicated from his drink he rises to his cousin and strikes his leg with the sword. In the delegation was a man from Banu Udal called al-Harith whose leg had been struck in a drink of theirs, in a house, he had recited poetry about a woman among them, so some of the people of that house rose and struck his leg with the sword. Al-Harith said when I heard it from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, I began to let down my garment to cover the blow on my leg, and Allah, Blessed and Exalted, had exposed it."
